/* ===== IELTS Speaking Room (B2–C1) and The Silk Road Heist (C1–C2) ===== */
/* question format: [text, options (correct first), explanation, meta?] */
/* Practice content in the style of the speaking test; not affiliated with or endorsed by IELTS. */

STORIES.ielts = { title: "IELTS Speaking Room", level: "B2–C1", unit: "Room",
units: [
{ id: "rules", name: "How the Test Works", az: "Test necə işləyir", ru: "Как устроен тест", emoji: "🎙️", img: null,
  note: "Welcome to the Speaking Room. The speaking test is a face-to-face conversation with an examiner and lasts 11–14 minutes. It has three parts: Part 1, short questions about familiar topics; Part 2, a long turn based on a cue card; Part 3, a deeper discussion. You are assessed on four criteria, each worth a quarter of your score: Fluency and Coherence (speaking smoothly and linking ideas), Lexical Resource (range and accuracy of vocabulary), Grammatical Range and Accuracy, and Pronunciation. Examiner tip: there are no “right” opinions — you are judged on how you speak, not on what you believe. Don't memorise answers: examiners notice, and it sounds unnatural. If you miss a question, simply ask: “Could you repeat that, please?” Speaking prompt: introduce yourself in three sentences — your name, where you're from and what you do.",
  qs: [
["How many criteria is your speaking score based on?", ["four", "three", "six"], "Four criteria: Fluency and Coherence, Lexical Resource, Grammatical Range and Accuracy, and Pronunciation. Each counts equally."],
["Which answer shows better Fluency and Coherence?", ["Well, I'd say I'm a morning person, mainly because I think more clearly before lunch.", "Morning. I like morning.", "Morning person… em… morning… yes, morning I like."], "The best answer flows smoothly and links the opinion to a reason with “mainly because”."],
["You didn't catch the examiner's question. What's the best thing to say?", ["Sorry, could you repeat the question, please?", "What?", "I don't know this topic."], "Asking politely for repetition is completely acceptable and doesn't lower your score."],
["Lexical ___ means the range and accuracy of your vocabulary.", ["Resource", "Source", "Resort"], "Lexical Resource is the vocabulary criterion: how many words you know and how well you use them.", {"t": "type", "alt": ["resource"]}],
["Why shouldn't you memorise long answers?", ["Examiners notice, and it sounds unnatural", "Memorised answers are always too short", "You may only speak for ten seconds"], "Memorised speech sounds rehearsed, and examiners are trained to spot it. Speak naturally instead."],
["Riddle: I'm the warm-up — short questions about your home, your work and your hobbies. Next room is Part ___.", ["1", "2", "3"], "Part 1! Next room: everyday questions about you."]]},

{ id: "part1", name: "Part 1: Everyday Talk", az: "1-ci hissə: gündəlik mövzular", ru: "Часть 1: повседневные темы", emoji: "🏙️", img: null,
  note: "Part 1 lasts 4–5 minutes. The examiner asks about you: your hometown, work or studies, hobbies. Aim for two or three sentences: an answer, a reason, an example. Examiner: “Where are you from?” Model answer: “I'm from Baku, the capital of Azerbaijan. It's a city of contrasts — the medieval Old City is just a stone's throw from ultra-modern skyscrapers like the Flame Towers. What I love most is the seaside boulevard, especially on summer evenings.” Examiner: “Do you work or study?” Model answer: “I'm currently studying international relations. I'm in my final year, so things are pretty hectic at the moment.” Examiner tip: avoid one-word answers, but don't give a speech either. Speaking prompt: what do you like most about your hometown, and why?",
  qs: [
["Examiner: “Do you like your hometown?” Which answer is the strongest?", ["Yes, very much — it's lively, and the seaside boulevard is perfect for evening walks.", "Yes.", "Yes, I like my hometown because I like my hometown very much."], "A short opinion plus a reason and a detail: exactly what Part 1 needs."],
["What's the ideal length for a Part 1 answer?", ["two or three sentences", "one word", "two full minutes"], "Answer, reason, example: two or three sentences keep you fluent without turning into a speech."],
["“A stone's throw from” means…", ["very close to", "far away from", "dangerous for"], "A stone's throw = a very short distance. The Old City is very close to the modern towers."],
["I'm in my final ___, so things are pretty hectic.", ["year", "class", "age"], "We say “I'm in my final year” at university or school.", {"t": "type"}],
["Fix the error: “I am living in Baku since 2010.”", ["I've been living in Baku since 2010.", "I living in Baku since 2010.", "I was live in Baku since 2010."], "With “since” + a starting point we need the present perfect (continuous): I've been living…"],
["Riddle: I'm a small card with a topic and four bullet points. You get one minute to plan, then you speak alone. Next room: the cue ___.", ["card", "phone", "ticket"], "The cue card! Next room: Part 2, your long turn."]]},

{ id: "part2", name: "Part 2: The Cue Card", az: "2-ci hissə: mövzu kartı", ru: "Часть 2: карточка с темой", emoji: "🗂️", img: null,
  note: "The examiner hands you a card: “Describe a place you enjoy visiting. You should say: where it is, how often you go there, what you do there, and explain why you enjoy it.” You have one minute to plan and may take notes — write keywords, not sentences. Then you speak for up to two minutes; the examiner will stop you. Model opening: “I'd like to talk about Sheki, a small town nestled in the foothills of the Caucasus. I try to go there at least once a year…” Examiner tip: use the bullet points as a map, but spend most of your time on “explain why” — that's where you show your range. If you run out of ideas, add a story: “I remember one time…” Speaking prompt: describe a person who has inspired you. Talk for two minutes.",
  qs: [
["How long do you have to plan your Part 2 talk?", ["one minute", "five minutes", "no time at all"], "You get exactly one minute to plan, with paper and a pencil."],
["What's the best way to use your planning time?", ["Write down keywords for each bullet point", "Write a full essay", "Sit silently and wait"], "Keywords are quick to write and easy to glance at while you speak."],
["Which opening sounds most natural?", ["I'd like to talk about Sheki, a small town nestled in the foothills of the Caucasus.", "My topic is place. The place is Sheki.", "Sheki. Okay. Sheki is place."], "“I'd like to talk about…” is a natural opener, and “nestled in the foothills” shows rich vocabulary."],
["If you run out of ideas, tell a short story: “I ___ one time…”", ["remember", "remind", "memory"], "“I remember one time…” is a natural way to start a personal story and keep talking.", {"t": "type"}],
["Which bullet point gives you the best chance to show your range?", ["explain why…", "where it is", "how often you go"], "“Explain why” invites reasons, feelings and complex grammar — the key to a higher band."],
["Riddle: after your talk, the examiner asks bigger questions — about society, not just about you. Next room is a two-way ___.", ["discussion", "dictation", "drawing"], "A discussion! Next room: Part 3."]]},

{ id: "part3", name: "Part 3: The Discussion", az: "3-cü hissə: müzakirə", ru: "Часть 3: дискуссия", emoji: "💬", img: null,
  note: "Part 3 lasts 4–5 minutes and moves from the personal to the abstract. After a cue card about a place, the examiner might ask: “Why do you think people are moving from villages to big cities?” A strong answer gives a position, reasons and an example, and may consider the other side. Model answer: “I'd put it down mainly to economic factors. Cities simply offer more jobs, and better-paid ones. Take Baku — a huge number of young people from the regions come here to study and then stay. That said, I think remote work could reverse the trend to some extent.” Useful frames: “It depends on…”, “On the whole…”, “One reason might be…”. Examiner tip: speculate freely — there is no wrong answer. Speaking prompt: is technology making people less sociable? Give two reasons and an example.",
  qs: [
["How is Part 3 different from Part 1?", ["The questions are more abstract and general", "The questions are only about your family", "You speak alone for two minutes"], "Part 3 moves from you to society: bigger, more abstract questions."],
["Examiner: “Why do people move to cities?” Which is the best Part 3 answer?", ["Mainly for economic reasons — cities offer more and better-paid jobs. Take Baku, for example…", "Because cities are good.", "I don't know, I live in a city."], "Position + reason + example: a well-developed answer."],
["“I'd put it down to…” means…", ["I think the cause is…", "I'd write it on paper…", "I'd refuse to answer…"], "Put something down to = explain the cause of something."],
["___ said, remote work could reverse the trend.", ["That", "This", "It"], "“That said” = however, despite this. A neat way to show the other side.", {"t": "type", "alt": ["that"]}],
["Which phrase introduces the other side of the argument?", ["On the other hand,", "For example,", "Firstly,"], "On the other hand introduces a contrasting view."],
["Riddle: “very big” becomes “enormous”, “very tired” becomes “exhausted”. Next room upgrades your ___.", ["vocabulary", "pronunciation", "handwriting"], "Vocabulary! Next room: Lexical Resource upgrades."]]},

{ id: "vocab", name: "Vocabulary Upgrades", az: "Lüğəti gücləndirmək", ru: "Прокачка словаря", emoji: "💎", img: null,
  note: "Lexical Resource rewards less common words, natural collocations and idiomatic language — used accurately. Compare: “Baku is a very nice city with many good buildings” and “Baku is a striking city with a wealth of impressive architecture.” Same idea, higher band. Collocations matter more than rare words: we say heavy traffic, not strong traffic; make a decision, not do a decision; a wide range of, not a big range of. Idioms should sound natural, not forced: “I was over the moon when I got my results” works; “It's raining cats and dogs, so I love my hometown” does not. Examiner tip: if you lack a word, paraphrase — “the thing you use to…” keeps you fluent. Speaking prompt: describe your favourite season using two collocations and one idiom.",
  qs: [
["Which is the correct collocation?", ["heavy traffic", "strong traffic", "big traffic"], "In English, traffic is heavy (or light), never strong."],
["Which is a higher-band version of “The food was very good”?", ["The food was absolutely delicious.", "The food was very very good.", "The food was good good."], "“Absolutely” + a strong adjective (delicious) sounds natural and precise."],
["Which idiom is used naturally?", ["I was over the moon when I got my results.", "I was over the moon because the traffic was heavy.", "It's raining cats and dogs, so I love my job."], "Over the moon = extremely happy. It fits good news, not bad traffic."],
["I need to ___ a decision about my university course.", ["make", "do", "take on"], "Make a decision is the standard collocation (“take a decision” is also British, but never “do”).", {"t": "type", "alt": ["take"]}],
["Choose the best word: “Baku has a ___ of impressive architecture.”", ["wealth", "money", "rich"], "A wealth of = a large amount of something good. A sophisticated, natural phrase."],
["Riddle: “If I had more time, I would travel.” “The tower was built in 2012.” Next room shows off your grammatical ___.", ["range", "silence", "spelling"], "Range! Next room: conditionals, passives and relative clauses."]]},

{ id: "grammar", name: "Grammar Range", az: "Qrammatik müxtəliflik", ru: "Грамматическое разнообразие", emoji: "🧩", img: null,
  note: "To reach band 7 and above, you need a mix of simple and complex structures, with many error-free sentences. Weave them in naturally. Conditionals: “If I hadn't moved to Baku, I wouldn't have met my best friends.” Passives: “The Flame Towers were completed in 2012 and are lit up every night.” Relative clauses: “My grandmother, who grew up in Ganja, taught me to cook.” Other useful patterns: “I've been learning English for six years”, “What I enjoy most is…”. Examiner tip: accuracy beats showing off — one correct complex sentence is better than three broken ones. If you slip, self-correct calmly: “She go— she goes there every week.” Examiners see that as control, not weakness. Speaking prompt: talk about a decision that changed your life, using one third conditional.",
  qs: [
["Which sentence is a correct third conditional?", ["If I hadn't moved to Baku, I wouldn't have met my best friends.", "If I didn't moved to Baku, I won't meet my best friends.", "If I wouldn't have moved, I hadn't met them."], "Third conditional: If + past perfect, would have + past participle."],
["Fix the error: “The Flame Towers built in 2012.”", ["The Flame Towers were built in 2012.", "The Flame Towers was build in 2012.", "The Flame Towers have build in 2012."], "The towers didn't build themselves: we need the passive, were + built."],
["My grandmother, ___ grew up in Ganja, taught me to cook.", ["who", "which", "whose"], "Who for people in a relative clause. The commas show it's extra information.", {"t": "type"}],
["Which sentence is both complex AND correct?", ["What I enjoy most about my job is working with people.", "What I enjoy most about my job it is working with people.", "I enjoy job. People good."], "A cleft sentence (What I enjoy most is…) adds emphasis — without an extra “it”."],
["You make a grammar mistake. What should you do?", ["Correct it quickly and carry on", "Stop and apologise for a long time", "Start the whole answer again"], "A quick self-correction shows control and keeps your fluency."],
["Riddle: all three parts, one examiner, fourteen minutes — no more practice rooms. Next is the full ___ test.", ["mock", "written", "listening"], "The mock test! Final room: the full test and feedback."]]},

{ id: "mock", name: "The Full Mock Test", az: "Tam sınaq imtahanı", ru: "Полный пробный экзамен", emoji: "🏆", img: null,
  note: "Time for the full mock test. Part 1: “Do you prefer mornings or evenings?” — “Definitely evenings. I'm not really a morning person, and Baku comes alive after sunset.” Part 2: you describe a teacher who inspired you. Part 3: “Should education be free for everyone?” Afterwards, your practice examiner gives feedback: “Your fluency is strong — you rarely hesitate and you link ideas well. Vocabulary: good collocations like ‘comes alive’, but avoid repeating ‘very’. Grammar: a wide range, though watch your articles — ‘the education’ should be ‘education’. Pronunciation: clear, with natural stress and intonation.” Estimated band: 7.0. Examiner tip: record yourself, listen back and choose one area to improve each week. Speaking prompt: answer all three parts aloud with a timer — then give yourself feedback.",
  qs: [
["Examiner: “Mornings or evenings?” Which answer is the most natural?", ["Definitely evenings — I'm not really a morning person.", "Evening is more better for me.", "Evenings. Mornings. Both."], "Clear opinion + natural phrase (a morning person). “More better” is a common error."],
["What was the examiner's feedback on grammar?", ["Watch your articles", "Never use conditionals", "Speak more slowly"], "The range was wide, but articles need attention: “the education” → “education”."],
["Fix the error: “The education should be free for everyone.”", ["Education should be free for everyone.", "The educations should be free for everyone.", "An education should is free for everyone."], "When we talk about education in general, we use no article."],
["Your fluency is strong — you rarely ___.", ["hesitate", "hesitation", "stop it"], "Hesitate = pause because you're unsure. Rarely hesitating is a sign of high fluency.", {"t": "type"}],
["What's the best way to keep improving?", ["Record yourself and focus on one area each week", "Memorise twenty model answers", "Only practise the day before the test"], "Regular, focused practice beats memorising. Listen back and fix one thing at a time."],
["Final: what is the examiner really listening for?", ["How well you communicate — fluency, vocabulary, grammar and pronunciation", "Whether your opinions are correct", "How many rare words you can memorise"], "Your ideas are never right or wrong — your English is what counts. You've completed the Speaking Room. Well done!"]]}
]};

STORIES.heist = { title: "The Silk Road Heist", level: "C1–C2", unit: "City",
units: [
{ id: "baku", name: "Baku: The Vanishing", az: "Bakı: yoxa çıxma", ru: "Баку: исчезновение", emoji: "🏰", img: null,
  note: "The call comes at 2:17 a.m. “It's gone,” whispers Dr Nigar Talibova, curator of the private exhibition at the Palace of the Shirvanshahs. By the time you arrive, the courtyard is bathed in floodlight and crawling with police. The Shirvan Codex — a fourteenth-century manuscript of star charts, its margins illuminated in lapis and gold — has vanished from a case that was never opened. No sooner had the last guest left than the alarm system fell silent for exactly four minutes. The codex's owner, the collector Kamran Vazirov, seems oddly unperturbed. “The insurance will cover it,” he shrugs, adjusting his cufflinks. Inspector Rauf Gasimov hands you, the art-crime consultant, the only clues: a single thread of raw silk and a scrap of paper bearing a geometric pattern you have seen before — in the windows of an old palace in Sheki.",
  qs: [
["What was stolen?", ["a fourteenth-century manuscript of star charts", "a golden astrolabe", "a silk carpet"], "The Shirvan Codex: a medieval manuscript of star charts, illuminated in lapis and gold."],
["No sooner had the last guest left ___ the alarm fell silent.", ["than", "when", "that"], "Inversion: No sooner had + subject + past participle… than… It stresses that one event followed the other immediately.", {"t": "type"}],
["Why might Vazirov's reaction seem suspicious?", ["He is oddly unperturbed by the loss", "He bursts into tears", "He accuses the curator"], "Unperturbed = calm, not worried. Strange for a man who has just lost a priceless manuscript."],
["“The courtyard is crawling with police” means…", ["it is full of police officers", "the police are moving slowly", "there are no police at all"], "To be crawling with = to be full of (people or creatures)."],
["Which word best describes a thief who is hard to find or catch?", ["elusive", "illusive", "allusive"], "Elusive = difficult to find or catch. Illusive relates to illusion; allusive to allusion."],
["Riddle: I'm a town of silk and sweet halva, where travellers once rested their camels in caravanserais. Next stop: ___.", ["Sheki", "Ganja", "Lankaran"], "Sheki! The pattern points to its famous palace windows."]]},

{ id: "sheki", name: "Sheki: The Glass Cipher", az: "Şəki: şüşə şifrə", ru: "Шеки: стеклянный шифр", emoji: "🪟", img: null,
  note: "Sheki greets you with the scent of walnut halva and woodsmoke. You lodge at the old caravanserai, where merchants once unloaded bales of silk beneath vaulted arches. Your real destination, however, is the Khan's Palace, whose shebeke windows — thousands of fragments of coloured glass fitted into a wooden lattice without a single nail or drop of glue — scatter light like shattered jewels. Had you not studied the pattern on the paper scrap, you would never have noticed it: in one panel, a handful of glass pieces are slightly brighter than the rest. Counting the red and blue fragments row by row, you transcribe a sequence of numbers that resolves into letters: ABANOTUBANI 6. Someone has taken considerable pains to hide a message in plain sight. Whoever it is, they either want you to follow — or want you to believe you are being clever.",
  qs: [
["What is remarkable about shebeke windows?", ["They are assembled without nails or glue", "They are made of plastic", "They are painted by machines"], "Shebeke: wooden lattice and coloured glass fitted together with no nails or glue."],
["“Had you not studied the pattern, you would never have noticed it.” This means…", ["You studied the pattern, so you noticed it", "You didn't study the pattern", "You noticed nothing"], "Inverted third conditional: Had you not = If you hadn't. In reality, you did study it — and noticed."],
["Someone has hidden the message in ___ sight.", ["plain", "clear", "open"], "In plain sight = openly visible, yet unnoticed. A classic thriller idiom.", {"t": "type"}],
["“To take considerable pains” means…", ["to make a great effort", "to suffer many injuries", "to take strong medicine"], "Take pains (to do something) = make a great effort to do it carefully."],
["Which sentence uses narrative tenses correctly?", ["By the time you reached the palace, the sun had already set.", "By the time you reached the palace, the sun has already set.", "By the time you had reached the palace, the sun already sets."], "Past perfect (had set) for the earlier action; past simple (reached) for the later one."],
["Riddle: my name comes from the old word for “warm”, and my bath district smells of sulphur. Next stop: ___.", ["Tbilisi", "Yerevan", "Tabriz"], "Tbilisi! Abanotubani is its famous sulphur-bath district."]]},

{ id: "tbilisi", name: "Tbilisi: The Double Agent", az: "Tbilisi: ikili agent", ru: "Тбилиси: двойной агент", emoji: "♨️", img: null,
  note: "In Abanotubani, the old bath district of Tbilisi, steam drifts from brick domes and the air hangs heavy with sulphur. In private room number six, a man is already waiting, wrapped in a white sheet: Levan Kipiani, a former museum guard turned informant, who insists he is on your side. “Vazirov's people asked me to find a buyer,” he admits, “but I'd sooner see the codex back in Baku.” Over glasses of tarragon lemonade, he tells you that the manuscript will surface at a closed-door sale in Istanbul. It all sounds rather too convenient. Later that night, from the balcony of your guesthouse in the old town, you glimpse Levan in the street below, deep in conversation with one of Vazirov's bodyguards. Is he playing both sides, or merely playing for time? Either way, you have the uneasy feeling that you are being led by the nose.",
  qs: [
["Who is Levan Kipiani?", ["a former museum guard turned informant", "Vazirov's brother", "a Georgian police chief"], "“A former museum guard turned informant”: X turned Y = someone who used to be X and has become Y."],
["“I'd sooner see the codex back in Baku” means…", ["I would prefer the codex to return to Baku", "I'll see the codex in Baku soon", "I saw the codex in Baku earlier"], "Would sooner = would rather, would prefer."],
["Why do you start to distrust Levan?", ["You see him talking to Vazirov's bodyguard", "He refuses to speak English", "He leaves the baths too early"], "He is deep in conversation with Vazirov's bodyguard — suspicious for someone “on your side”."],
["You have the feeling that you are being led by the ___.", ["nose", "hand", "eye"], "Lead someone by the nose = control someone completely, make them do what you want.", {"t": "type"}],
["A double agent is someone who…", ["secretly works for two opposing sides", "has two passports", "does twice as much work"], "A double agent pretends to work for one side while actually serving the other."],
["Riddle: I'm one of the oldest covered markets in the world, with thousands of shops under one roof. Next stop: the Grand ___ in Istanbul.", ["Bazaar", "Palace", "Hotel"], "The Grand Bazaar! The secret auction awaits."]]},

{ id: "istanbul", name: "Istanbul: The Grand Bazaar", az: "İstanbul: Qapalı Çarşı", ru: "Стамбул: Гранд-базар", emoji: "🕌", img: null,
  note: "Beneath the painted vaults of the Grand Bazaar, past lamp-sellers and carpet dealers, a narrow staircase leads to an old han where Selim Arkan, an antiques dealer of dubious reputation, holds his invitation-only auctions. You enter posing as an agent for a wealthy private collector. The room is thick with cigar smoke and whispered bids. When the codex is unveiled, a hush falls. Bidding climbs to two million dollars before the hammer falls — to an anonymous telephone bidder. Yet something nags at you. Examining the pages under your loupe minutes earlier, you had noticed that the gold leaf lay perfectly flat, with none of the fine cracks that six centuries should have left. Were the manuscript genuine, the pigment would have faded at the edges. It is a counterfeit — and a masterful one. And Selim's receipt bears the watermark of a paper mill in Venice.",
  qs: [
["How did you get into the auction?", ["by posing as an agent for a collector", "by bribing the guards", "by climbing through a window"], "Pose as = pretend to be someone you are not."],
["“Were the manuscript genuine, the pigment would have faded.” This is…", ["an inverted conditional (= If the manuscript were genuine…)", "a passive sentence", "a question about the manuscript"], "Were + subject replaces If + subject + were: a formal, literary conditional."],
["It is a ___ — a fake made to deceive.", ["counterfeit", "original", "replica"], "Counterfeit = a fake made to look genuine and deceive people.", {"t": "type", "alt": ["forgery", "fake"]}],
["What made you suspect that the codex was fake?", ["The gold leaf had none of the cracks of age", "The pages were written in Turkish", "It was far too heavy"], "Six-hundred-year-old gold leaf should be covered in fine cracks. This one was perfect — too perfect."],
["“An antiques dealer of dubious reputation” is…", ["a dealer who may not be trustworthy", "a very famous dealer", "a recently retired dealer"], "Dubious = doubtful, possibly dishonest."],
["Riddle: my streets are made of water, my bridges number in the hundreds, and my masks hide many faces. Next stop: ___.", ["Venice", "Amsterdam", "Vienna"], "Venice! The watermark leads to the forger."]]},

{ id: "venice", name: "Venice: The Forger's Workshop", az: "Venesiya: saxtakarın emalatxanası", ru: "Венеция: мастерская фальсификатора", emoji: "🎭", img: null,
  note: "The watermark leads you to a crumbling palazzo in Cannaregio, where the canal laps at the steps and the workshop reeks of rabbit-skin glue and linseed oil. Matteo Brenna, a gifted restorer who once worked for great museums, now makes a lucrative living from forgeries he insists on calling “homages”. He neither denies nor confirms anything until you mention the word prison. Then he crumbles. “I made the copy two years ago,” he sighs. “The client insisted that it be perfect — down to the wormholes.” Two years ago: long before the theft. Only someone with unrestricted access to the original could have commissioned such a copy. Reluctantly, he produces the paperwork: an invoice from a Swiss trust called Kervan Holdings, paid in full. Not only had the theft been planned well in advance, but the codex stolen in Baku had been a fake all along.",
  qs: [
["When was the copy made?", ["two years before the theft", "on the night of the theft", "after the Istanbul auction"], "Matteo made it two years earlier — so the theft was planned long in advance."],
["Matteo ___ denies nor confirms anything.", ["neither", "either", "not"], "Neither… nor… = not one and not the other.", {"t": "type"}],
["“The client insisted that it ___ perfect.”", ["be", "is", "will"], "Subjunctive: after insist/demand/require that, we use the bare infinitive (be), even in the past."],
["“Not only had the theft been planned, but…” What does the inversion do here?", ["It adds emphasis and a formal, dramatic tone", "It turns the sentence into a question", "It puts the sentence in the future"], "Negative adverbials at the start (Not only, Never, Little) trigger inversion for dramatic emphasis."],
["Who could have commissioned the copy?", ["someone with unrestricted access to the original", "any tourist in Venice", "one of Selim Arkan's customers"], "To copy every wormhole, the forger needed the original in front of him for a long time."],
["Riddle: Big Ben strikes, the gavel falls, and the Thames flows past the auction rooms. Next stop: ___.", ["London", "Paris", "Geneva"], "London! A grand auction house — and a twist."]]},

{ id: "london", name: "London: The Twist", az: "London: gözlənilməz dönüş", ru: "Лондон: неожиданный поворот", emoji: "🔨", img: null,
  note: "Ashcombe & Vale, a discreet auction house in Mayfair, has listed a “recently rediscovered fourteenth-century Shirvani astronomical manuscript” for its spring sale. The catalogue boasts an impeccable provenance: it has supposedly belonged for decades to a Swiss trust — Kervan Holdings. You request a private viewing. This time the gold leaf is crazed with fine cracks, the vellum is soft with age, and a tiny tear on folio twelve matches a photograph in the Baku archives. It is the real thing. Then the penny drops. Vazirov had swapped the original for Brenna's copy, exhibited the fake, staged its theft to claim the insurance, and was now quietly selling the original through his own trust. The trail through Sheki, Tbilisi and Istanbul had been nothing but a wild goose chase, designed to keep you looking east while the real prize slipped away to the west.",
  qs: [
["What does “provenance” mean?", ["the documented history of an object's ownership", "the price of an object", "the country where an object is sold"], "Provenance = the record of where an object came from and who has owned it. Crucial in the art world."],
["How do you know the London manuscript is genuine?", ["A tiny tear matches an archive photograph", "The auctioneer swears it is", "It has a certificate from Selim Arkan"], "Physical evidence: the tear on folio twelve matches the archive photo, and the gold leaf shows real age."],
["Then the penny ___ — you finally understand.", ["drops", "falls", "rolls"], "The penny drops = you suddenly understand something.", {"t": "type", "alt": ["dropped"]}],
["What was Vazirov's plan?", ["to claim insurance on the fake and secretly sell the original", "to donate the codex to a museum", "to destroy the manuscript"], "A double profit: insurance money for the “stolen” fake plus the sale of the real codex."],
["“A wild goose chase” is…", ["a long, pointless search", "a hunting trip", "a fast car race"], "A wild goose chase = a search that is hopeless or deliberately misleading."],
["Riddle: three towers shaped like flames light up the Baku night with fire and colour. The final scene takes place at the ___ Towers.", ["Flame", "Maiden", "Twin"], "The Flame Towers! Back to Baku for the reveal."]]},

{ id: "flame", name: "Baku: The Reveal", az: "Bakı: həqiqət üzə çıxır", ru: "Баку: развязка", emoji: "🔥", img: null,
  note: "Back in Baku, the Flame Towers blaze against the night sky. In Vazirov's penthouse, the collector is pouring cognac when you walk in with Inspector Gasimov and Dr Talibova. “You've had quite a tour,” he smiles. “Sheki, Tbilisi, Istanbul, Venice, London. Pity it was all for nothing.” Then the lift doors open and Levan steps out. “Not for nothing,” he says. Far from betraying you, he had been feeding Vazirov false information all along, working quietly with the inspector — and it was he who traced the anonymous phone bid in Istanbul to Vazirov's own number. Little did the collector know that Ashcombe & Vale had frozen the London sale that very morning. Vazirov's smile fades. “If I had sold it to a museum,” he murmurs, “I'd be a hero now.” A week later, the Shirvan Codex returns to the Palace of the Shirvanshahs, where it belongs.",
  qs: [
["What was Levan really doing?", ["feeding Vazirov false information while working with the police", "selling the codex to Selim Arkan", "forging documents in Venice"], "Far from betraying you = the opposite of betraying you. Levan was on your side all along."],
["“Little did the collector know that…” means…", ["The collector had no idea that…", "The collector knew a little about…", "The collector wanted to know…"], "Little did + subject + verb = inversion meaning “had no idea at all”."],
["“If I had sold it to a museum, I'd be a hero now.” This is a…", ["mixed conditional (past cause, present result)", "first conditional", "zero conditional"], "Mixed conditional: If + past perfect (past), would + infinitive (now)."],
["The codex returns to the palace, where it ___.", ["belongs", "lives", "stays"], "Where it belongs = in its rightful place.", {"t": "type"}],
["Choose the most precise word: “At last, Vazirov's elaborate scheme ___.”", ["unravelled", "unwrapped", "unfolded"], "A scheme or plan unravels = falls apart, is exposed. Unwrap is for presents."],
["Final: who was really behind the theft of the Shirvan Codex?", ["Kamran Vazirov, the codex's own owner", "Levan Kipiani", "Dr Nigar Talibova"], "The owner staged the theft of his own fake to collect the insurance and sell the original. Case closed, consultant!"]]}
]};

